      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.104,
            0.295,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "COORDINATE GEOMETRY"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.863,
            0.104,
            0.898,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "101"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.14,
            0.555,
            0.16
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, QT = 2 units and $\\mathrm{PT} = \\mathrm{RS} = 2$ units."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.168,
            0.562,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": "Now, using the Pythagoras theorem, we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.216,
            0.437,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} PQ ^{2} = &  PT ^{2} +  OT ^{2} \\\\= & 2^{2} + 2^{2} = 8.\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.274,
            0.135,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.272,
            0.433,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": "PQ = \\sqrt[r]{2}   units",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.302,
            0.562,
            0.344
          ],
          "content": "How will we find the distance between two points in two different quadrants?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.351,
            0.564,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": "Consider the points P(6, 4) and Q(–5, –3) (see Fig. 7.4). Draw QS perpendicular to the x-axis. Also draw a perpendicular PT from the point P on QS (extended) to meet y-axis at the point R."
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.572,
            0.146,
            0.901,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.688,
                0.403,
                0.777,
                0.424
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.3"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_chart_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.252,
            0.461,
            0.75,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.455,
                0.774,
                0.543,
                0.794
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.4"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.82,
            0.591,
            0.84
          ],
          "content": "Then PT = 11 units and QT = 7 units. (Why?)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.846,
            0.902,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": "Using the Pythagoras Theorem to the right triangle PTQ, we get $PQ = \\sqrt{11^{2} + 7^{2}} = \\sqrt{170}$ units."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
